nd 4 and columns 0 and 4 fully coloured.\n- Pair 1: the 8-block (rows 6–11, cols 4–13) keeps its whole border.\n\nInside the border, the cleared cells alternate like a chessboard. Let (dy, dx) be a cell's offset from the rectangle's top-left corner. Then:\n- Pair 1, 1-block: cleared cells are (1,1), (1,3), (2,2), (3,1), (3,3). Each has an even dy+dx.\n- Pair 1, 8-block: (7,5) has offset (1,1), so the sum is 2, even, and it is cleared. (7,12) has offset (1,8), sum 9, odd, and it stays 8. This matches the output row `8 0 8 0 8 0 8 0 8 8`.\n- Pair 3, 8-block (rows 9–13, cols 10–15): (11,14) has offset (2,4), even, cleared. (12,11) has offset (3,1), even, cleared. (12,12) has offset (3,2), odd, kept. This matches the output.\n\nBecause the parity is measured from each rectangle's own corner, the rule needs no absolute coordinates. That matters here, since the test grid (19×19) is a size no train pair uses.\n\n**Rule.** For every single-colour object, take its bounding box. Set every interior cell (not on the box edge) whose (dy+dx) is even to the background colour.\n\n**Applying it to the test.** The test has four rectangles:\n\n| Colour | Size | Interior | First cleared cell |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8 | 9×8 | 7×6 | offset (1,1) |\n| 7 | 7×8 | 5×6 | offset (1,1) |\n| 6 | 8×11 | 6×9 | offset (1,1) |\n| 4 | 6×5 | 4×3 | offset (1,1) |\n\nIn each one the checkerboard starts at the interior corner (offset (1,1)) and alternates from there, while the outer ring is left untouched. I emit one stage per rectangle, so each stage is one whole object being patterned.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    for o in objects(g, bg=bg):\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        for y in range(y0 + 1, y1):\n            for x in range(x0 + 1, x1):\n                if (y - y0 + x - x0) % 2 == 0 and out[y, x] == o[\"colour\"]:\n                    out[y, x] = bg\n        emit(out, f\"Checkerboard the interior of the colour-{o['colour']} rectangle \"\n                  f\"(box {y0},{x0}-{y1},{x1}): interior cells with even offset sum from its corner become background; border kept.\")\n    emit(out, \"All rectangles patterned; final output.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the grids are made of.** Every input is a black background holding a few solid, axis-aligned rectangles, each a single colour."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Inside the border, the cleared cells alternate like a chessboard."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In each one the checkerboard starts at the interior corner (offset (1,1)) and alternates from there, while the outer ring is left untouched."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Set every interior cell (not on the box edge) whose (dy+dx) is even to the background colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"| Colour | Size | Interior | First cleared cell |"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In each one the checkerboard starts at the interior corner (offset (1,1)) and alternates from there, while the outer ring is left untouched."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Because the parity is measured from each rectangle's own corner, the rule needs no absolute coordinates."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Rule.** For every single-colour object, take its bounding box."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"da0ad4866ee86254d05cafec450d6897b1d83dfb91bd28ffcf0fb0bdd64f5077","task_sha256":"02fa931245feb68ea9d5e57d0b6034e74edc5905a01b2476aa6b5265755feff1","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
